# Retrieving Soft Deleted Relationships on Demand in Laravel

When working with soft deletes in Laravel, the default behavior of Eloquent relationships is to filter out records that have been marked for deletion. This is usually what you want; it keeps your data clean and prevents accidental access to sensitive information. However, there are specific scenarios where you need to inspect a relationship's history for a single instance without permanently altering the model's global scope or creating a permanent change to the class definition.

The standard approach shown in many tutorials involves adding `withTrashed()` directly to the relationship method within the Model:

```php
public function contacts(){
    return $this->hasMany('App\Contacts', 'user_id', 'id')->withTrashed();
}
```

While this works, it changes the behavior of the `contacts` property for every instance of the `User` model. If your application relies on soft deletes to hide past records by default, adding `withTrashed()` globally might violate that requirement or complicate testing. You likely want a solution that is scoped strictly to a specific controller action or function call, ensuring that trashed rows are returned only for that one request.

To achieve this without modifying the Model class, you must inject the scope directly into your query builder instance at runtime. When you access `$user->contacts`, Eloquent returns an `HasMany` relationship object. You can manipulate the underlying query before executing it by accessing the `getRelationExist()` method or, more commonly, by using the `withTrashed()` method on the relationship collection itself after eager loading logic is defined.

However, a cleaner and more robust pattern for this specific "one-off" requirement is to apply the scope to the query when resolving the relationship in the controller. You can do this by accessing the underlying relation object and calling `getRelationExist` or simply chaining the method if you are eager loading. But since `contacts` returns a collection, the most direct way to include trashed items for just this instance is to use the `withTrashed()` method on the relationship property before resolving it fully, or by utilizing the query builder's scope capability.

Actually, the most precise way to handle this in a controller without touching the model is to access the relation and force the scope on the specific query execution. Here is how you can approach it:

```php
$user = User::findOrFail($id);

// Access the relationship and apply the trashed scope immediately
$allContacts = $user->contacts()->withTrashed()->get();

return response()->json($user, 200);
```

By chaining `->withTrashed()` onto the `$user->contacts` property within the controller, you are essentially invoking the relationship method dynamically. This creates a fresh query builder instance that includes the soft delete scope for this specific request only. The global model definition remains untouched, so other parts of your application continue to respect the default filtering behavior.

It is important to note how Eloquent handles scopes. When you call `withTrashed()` on a relationship, it adds `?where("deleted_at" IS NULL)` to the query's exclusion logic and removes that filter for this execution. This ensures that the database query retrieves rows where the `deleted_at` column contains a timestamp, alongside those where it is null.

One potential pitfall when debugging these queries is ensuring you are inspecting the actual SQL or using Laravel's query logs. Sometimes, developers assume that accessing `$user->contacts` returns the raw collection immediately, but Eloquent might be optimizing eager loading differently depending on how the data is consumed. If you are passing the model to a view or serializing it, ensure you aren't accidentally relying on the filtered state in subsequent logic unless you explicitly reload with the scope applied again.

If your architecture requires complex relationship logic where simple chaining isn't enough, consider using the `withTrashed()` scope within a local scope defined in the model, but that brings us back to modifying the class. For strict one-off needs, runtime chaining remains the most effective strategy. It preserves data integrity while providing the necessary visibility into historical relationships for auditing or administrative purposes.

For more advanced patterns involving soft deletes and relationship scopes, you might find additional utility in resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com), which often covers edge cases in Eloquent that aren't immediately obvious from the default documentation.
